  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1015",
      "html": "<p><strong>Scientific name:</strong> Leptodoras cataniai Sabaj Pérez, 2005</p><p><strong>Common name:</strong> Slank Musemalle (Denmark)</p><p><strong>Etymology:</strong> Leptodoras: from the Greek leptos, meaning slender, and doras, meaning skin (also a word commonly used in forming generic names for doradids); in reference to the shape. Named in honor of David Catania, for his dedicated service to the ichthyological community since 1985 as Collection Manager of Fishes, California Academy of Sciences.</p><p><strong>Size:</strong> 194 mm (7.6 in) SL.</p><p><strong>IUCN Red List category:</strong> Least Concern. Last assessed 2020.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1015",
      "html": "<p>South America: Rio Negro/Casiquiare Canal in Brazil and Venezuela.</p><p><strong>Type locality:</strong> Río Casiquiare from mouth of río Pamoni to 4 kilometers below mouth, west of Pamoni, 2°48'N, 65°57'W, Amazonas, Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/leptodoras_cataniai.htm",
      "html": "<p>South America: Rio Negro/Casiquiare Canal in Brazil and Venezuela. Type locality: Venezuela, Amazonas, río Casiquiare from mouth of río Pamoni to 4 km below mouth, west of Pamoni (2º48'N, 65º57'W).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/leptodoras_cataniai.htm",
      "html": "<p>Provide a sandy substrate as it likes to dig looking for morsels.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/leptodoras_cataniai.htm",
      "html": "<p>Tablet, pellet foods and also frozen foods such as bloodworm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1015",
      "html": "<p>Unreported in the aquarium. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/leptodoras_cataniai.htm",
      "html": "<p>Description: Dorsal spines (total): 1; dorsal soft rays (total): 6; anal soft rays: 14-17. Distal portion of the upper labial extension distinctly elongated, forming acute tip which sometimes appears as an enlarged terminal fimbriae.</p><p>The first three thumbnail images depict a very similar looking species reported to be from the Rio Nanay in Peru, which flows through Iquitos. This species along with another from the Amazonas have been termed 'Nanay Form' and 'Amazon Form'. This excludes the true species, which is from the Rio Negro. There are differences in oral-hood morphology in all three. Three groups were parsed (Sabaj, 2005): L. cataniai sensu stricto (Rio Negro and Casiquiare canal), L. cf. cataniai Amazon form (Amazonas basin excluding Nanay and Negro) and L. cf. cataniai Nanay form (specimens largely from Río Nanay, Peru).</p><p>Etymology: The specific name cataniai honours David Catania, for his dedicated service to the ichthyological community since 1985 as Collection Manager of Fishes, California Academy of Sciences.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1015",
      "html": "<p>Neotropical Ichthyology v. 3 (no. 4), pp 667, Figs. 4g, 5g, 6b, 18e and 22.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/doradidae/leptodoras_cataniai.htm",
      "html": "<p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628. Sabaj, M.H. 2005 Taxonomic assessment of Leptodoras (Siluriformes: Doradidae) with descriptions of three new species. Neotrop. Ichthyol. 3(4):637-678. The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
